Mr. Albert:

You have written to me three times, and each time you have steered this 
matter informally. I would like to know whether that is how your office 
carries out its statutory duties. I have prepared the attached public 
records request for your office. To whom do I submit it?

Respectfully,

*Robert Samuel White*
rswfire.com <https://rswfire.com/>
oprdvolunteerabuse.org <https://oprdvolunteerabuse.org/>
autonomyrealms.com <https://autonomyrealms.com/>

On 10/23/26 08:11, ALBERT Todd * PRA wrote:
>
> Hi Mr. White,
>
> I do not have enough information to determine if any of your four 
> requests qualify for facilitated dispute resolution. Moreover, ORS 
> 192.468 states, “Consistent with ORS 192.464 and rules adopted 
> thereunder, the Public Records Advocate possesses sole discretion over 
> the conduct of facilitated dispute resolution sessions.”
>
> Facilitated dispute resolution is merely mediation. I do not have the 
> authority to order outcomes in public records requests. As such, 
> whether we are assisting through facilitated dispute resolution or 
> other, less formal forms of mediation, I need to understand the 
> parameters of a disputed request, and have the cooperation of all parties.
>
> At the moment, I have none of these things. As mentioned previously, I 
> am also one of two people in a state agency that received nearly 400 
> individual requests for assistance such as yours last year while 
> striving to address them all in a timely matter. Please keep in mind 
> that mediating disputed requests is only one of the forms of 
> assistance we offer on public records matters and, as such, are 
> engaged in those other duties too.
>
> Therefore, we assist requesters with one disputed public records 
> request at a time to assure equitable access to all of those seeking 
> our services. We also require answers to our questions and agreeable 
> interactions that permit the free flow of information.
>
> With this in mind, are you interested in working with us along the 
> terms I outlined? If so, with which request do you wish to begin?
>
> On the other hand, if you find these terms unacceptable, then we will 
> not be able to work with you further and I wish you the best of luck 
> in your endeavors.
>
> Kind regards,
>
> Todd Albert
>
> Public Records Advocate
>
> Office of the Public Records Advocate
>
> mobile: (503) 871-9036
>
> www.oregon.gov/pra <http://www.oregon.gov/pra>
>
>
